FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducer, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    my %c;
    @c{qw(b a c)}++;
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($c{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %c), "\n";   # perl a=u b=u c=1   PCL (empty line)
    my @a = (1, 1, 1);
    @a[0, 2]++;
    print "@a\n";                                                           # perl 1 1 2         PCL 1 1 1
    my %e;
    @e{qw(p q)} += 5;
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($e{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %e), "\n";   # perl p=u q=5       PCL (empty line)
    my %f;
    ++@f{qw(m n)};
    print join(' ', map { "$_=" . ($f{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %f), "\n";   # perl m=u n=1       PCL (empty line)

perl: the slice is an lvalue (every element is vivified, as for any lvalue slice) and the scalar operator applies to its LAST element.  The everyday idiom is `@seen{@list}++` / `@count{LIST}++` to make a set of keys (the values do not matter, `keys` does): PCL leaves the hash EMPTY, silently.

WHERE (emitted Lisp): `(p-post++ (p-hslice %c @k))` -- the increment is applied to the rvalue slice's result, so neither the vivification nor the write reaches the hash.  Expected: vivify every element of the slice as an lvalue, then modify the last element's place.

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): the program becomes identical to perl when the slice increment is spelled `$c{$_}++ for @$_` (the per-key loop), so it is the only cause.

THE PROGRAM: 0243-Common-sorted-list collects the union of several lists as hash keys with a slice increment and prints the sorted keys: PCL prints nothing.

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642) === the SAME cause with `undef` as the operator: Rosetta 0808-Ludic-numbers makes a set of keys with `undef @h{LIST}` (perl: every key vivified, the last undef'd) and PCL leaves the hash empty.
NEUTRAL REPRODUCER (run on the tree):
    my %h;
    undef @h{qw(a b c)};
    print join(",", sort keys %h), "\n";                                     # perl a,b,c     PCL (empty)
    my %g = (a => 1, b => 2);
    undef @g{qw(a b)};
    print join(",", map { "$_=" . ($g{$_} // 'u') } sort keys %g), "\n";    # perl a=1,b=u   PCL a=1,b=2
  emitted: `(p-undef (p-hslice %h "a" "b" "c"))` -- the same rvalue-slice operand as `(p-post++ (p-hslice ...))` above.  The rule for the fix brief: a SLICE as the operand of a modifying scalar operator (`++` `--` `op=` `undef`) is an lvalue list -- vivify every element, act on the last.  Rewriting the line as a per-key `$h{$_} = undef for LIST` makes 0808 identical to perl -- its only cause.
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 0243, 0808)

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642) === Rosetta 0887-N-body-problem: the one-element SLICE spelling of an element as the LEFT side of `+=` / `-=` (`@$r[$i] += $d`, a common beginner spelling of `$r->[$i]`) -- every update is lost, so the bodies never move.
NEUTRAL REPRODUCER (run on the tree):
    my $v = [1, 2, 3];
    @$v[1] += 10;  @$v[2] -= 1;
    print "@$v\n";                 # perl 1 12 2     PCL 1 2 3
Rewriting the nine `@$r[$i] op=` updates as `$$r[$i] op=` makes 0887 identical to perl -- its only cause.
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 0243, 0808, 0887)


=== DONE (s543v) === (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-28; WIP 1 029799a0 on base 38420fae, generation v2-16800)
THE MEASUREMENT (perl 5.40.3, the whole operator family on a hash slice and an array slice): `++` `--` (both fixities, the VALUE too: postfix -- of undef is undef), every numeric / bitwise / string `OP=`, `||=` `&&=` `//=`, `.=`, `undef` -- each vivifies EVERY element of the slice (an absent key holds undef, an array grows; a negative non-last index before the start dies non-creatable) and acts on the LAST one, whose result is the expression's value; a TIED container sees only the last key's FETCH / STORE (no EXISTS, nothing for the others); an EMPTY slice dies "Modification of a read-only value attempted".  `%h{...}++` and `(LIST)[i]++` are perl COMPILE errors (out of scope, rule 9).  `my $v = undef @h{...}` leaves the hash unchanged in perl (a TARGMY op-tree quirk) -- not matched, rare.  Controls already right on the base: chomp / chop (every element), `=~ s///` / `tr` (the last), `\ @h{...}`, `for (@h{...})`, `delete`, `exists`, `local`.
THE FIX (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp, emission unchanged): `%p-slice-operand-form` -- the slice form, bare or in its `p-list-scalar` / `p-slice-result` wrap, becomes the LAST element's own place with the container and the subscript list evaluated ONCE -- asked by the existing entry of every operator family: `%p-incdec-once-form` (the four ++/-- macros), `%store-back-form` (the compound family), `%p-elem-place-form` (`||=` `&&=` `//=`), `p-.=`, and a new `p-undef` compiler macro (the p-join / p-map precedent).  The run-time halves `%p-hslice-lvalue-last` / `%p-aslice-lvalue-last` vivify the other elements without promoting a cell and die on an empty slice.  ir-spec §3.2e3.
NEIGHBOUR FIXED (same probe): Parser2 `_sub_writes_args` read a written `@_[LIST]` slice as a READ, so `@_[0,1] = ...` / `+= 3` / `++` in a callee never reached the caller (it now asks the `$_[N]` predicate `_args_elem_written`).  The wide A/B shows one real site: core App::Prove's `_shuffle` (`@_[$i, $j] = @_[$j, $i]`) shuffled a copy.
